  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In this problem we'll use a periodic function to describe the number of
        hours of daylight in Grand Rapids throughout the year.
        In particular, <m>t</m> will denote time as the number of days since
        January 1 and <m>H</m> will be the number of hours of daylight.
        So we want to describe a function <m>H=f(t)</m>.
        Here are some (approximate) facts:
        <ul>
          <li>
            On the vernal equinox (usually March 21, <m>t=80</m> ), there are 12
            hours of daylight.
            The same happens on the autumnal equinox (usually September 21).
          </li>

          <li>
            On the summer equinox (June 21), there are 15 hours of daylight.
          </li>

          <li>
            On the winter equinox (December 21), there are 9 hours of daylight.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <em>Pro tip:</em> I asked
        <url href="https://www.wolframalpha.com/">Wolfram Alpha</url> what day
        of the year March 21 is, and it said 81, so I got <m>t=80</m> days since
        Jan 1.
      </p>

      <p>
        We'll build the function <m>f(t)</m> piece by piece below starting with
        <m>\sin(t)</m> and transforming it.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the period <m>p</m> of <m>f(t)</m>?
          Explain how you know.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task  workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the midline <m>m</m> and what is the amplitude <m>a</m>?
          Explain how you got those.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give a rough sketch of how the final graph of <m>f(t)</m> should
          behave on the axes below.
          Include a short sentence about why you chose that particular shape, or
          starting point, or ... (I'm looking for your thought process, not a final equation here.)
        </p>

        <image width="55%" margins="0% 45%">
          <shortdescription>
            An empty graph to plot a rough sketch of f(t).
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-hw6-c">
            <diagram dimensions="(600,200)" margins="10">
              <definition>
                f(x)=-2*(-1)^1/(1*pi)*sin(1*x*pi)-2*(-1)^2/(2*pi)*sin(2*x*pi)-2*(-1)^3/(3*pi)*sin(3*pi*x)
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-20,-5,360,20)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,40,360),(0,5,20))" hlabels="(0,40,360)" vlabels="(0,5,20)" xlabel="t" ylabel="f(t)"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now transforming <m>\sin(t)</m>.
          What is the value of <m>k</m> so that <m>\sin(kt)</m> has the proper
          period as shown below.
        </p>

        <image width="55%" margins="0% 45%">
          <shortdescription>A graph of sin(kt).</shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-hw6-d">
            <diagram dimensions="(600,200)" margins="10">
              <definition>
                f(x)=sin(2*pi/360*x)
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-20,-5,360,20)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,40,360),(0,5,20))" hlabels="(0,40,360)" vlabels="(0,5,20)" xlabel="t" />
                <graph function="f"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          How can you modify <m>\sin(kt)</m> to have the proper amplitude?
          (The resulting graph is shown below.)
        </p>

        <image width="55%" margins="0% 45%">
          <shortdescription>A graph of a*sin(kt).</shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-hw6-e">
            <diagram dimensions="(600,200)" margins="10">
              <definition>
                f(x)=3*sin(2*pi/360*x)
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-20,-5,360,20)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,40,360),(0,5,20))" hlabels="(0,40,360)" vlabels="(0,5,20)" xlabel="t"/>
                <graph function="f"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          How can you modify the function from part (e) to have the proper
          midline?
          (The resulting graph is shown below.)
        </p>

        <image width="55%" margins="0% 45%">
          <shortdescription>
            A graph of the sine function with the correct amplitude, period, and
            midline to account for the context of the problem.
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-hw6-f">
            <diagram dimensions="(600,200)" margins="10">
              <definition>
                f(x)=3*sin(2*pi/360*x)+12
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-20,-5,360,20)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,40,360),(0,5,20))" hlabels="(0,40,360)" vlabels="(0,5,20)" xlabel="t"/>
                <graph function="f"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.3in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is one final modification you can make to part (f) to obtain
          <m>f(t)</m>?
          (The resulting graph is shown below.)
        </p>

        <image width="55%" margins="0% 45%">
          <shortdescription>
            A graph of the sine function with appropriate parameters to account
            for the context of the problem.
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-hw6-g">
            <diagram dimensions="(600,200)" margins="10">
              <definition>
                f(x)=3*sin(2*pi/360*(x-80))+12
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-20,-5,360,20)">
                <grid-axes decorations="yes" spacings="((0,40,360),(0,5,20))" hlabels="(0,40,360)" vlabels="(0,5,20)" xlabel="t" ylabel="f(t)"/>
                <graph function="f"/>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What does your function predict for the number of hours of daylight on
          October 9 (of 2024, which is a leap year)?
          (When you compute this <ndash/> should your input be in degrees or
          radians?) Explain what you did to find this prediction.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use Desmos to verify that your function is correct.
          If you think it is what it is supposed to be, tell me why, and if 
          not then tell me what is different from what you think it should look like and
          where you think something might have went funky.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>